      Web3 Trading Behavior vs Market Sentiment



Submitted by: Srushti Pramod Bhilare
Role: Data Science Candidate
Date: 27-01-2026


Objective
The objective of this analysis is to understand how trader behavior such as 
profitability, risk exposure, trading volume, and leverage changes under 
different market sentiment conditions categorized as Fear and Greed.

Data Overview

This analysis uses two datasets:

1. Hyperliquid Historical Trader Data containing trade-level information 
   such as execution price, trade size, leverage, PnL, and fees.

2. Bitcoin Fear & Greed Index dataset that classifies market sentiment 
   into Fear and Greed on a daily basis.


Key columns used in analysis:
- closed_pnl (Profit and Loss)
- risk_exposure
- trade_volume
- leverage
- Classification (Market Sentiment)

Figure 1: Trade Volume Distribution

This chart shows the distribution of trading volume across all trades.
High concentration of volume indicates periods of aggressive trading activity,
mostly observed during Greed sentiment.

Figure 2: Daily PnL Trend

The daily PnL trend highlights increased volatility during Greed periods,
while Fear periods show relatively stable but lower returns.

Key Findings

• Traders tend to take higher risk and larger position sizes during Greed sentiment.
• Fear periods show lower trading volume but comparatively better risk-adjusted returns.
• Excessive leverage during Greed leads to higher losses and volatility.
• Risk buckets clearly separate profitable and non-profitable trading behavior.


Conclusion

Market sentiment plays a significant role in shaping trader behavior.
Greed-driven markets encourage aggressive and high-risk trades, often 
resulting in unstable returns. In contrast, Fear periods favor disciplined 
trading with controlled risk.

Understanding these patterns can help traders design smarter strategies 
by adjusting leverage, position sizing, and risk exposure based on market sentiment.

